-- Q65: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A binding contract formed Thursday because acceptance is effective upon dispatch, while revocation requires actual receipt by the offeree","No contract formed because the revocation was mailed before the acceptance, making the mailbox rule inapplicable to this sequence","The contract formed Wednesday when the revocation was mailed, because revocations take priority over acceptances under common law","The contract formed Friday when Baker received the revocation, because all contractual communications are effective only upon receipt"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the common law mailbox rule (Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 63), an acceptance is effective upon dispatch while a revocation is effective only upon receipt. Baker mailed the acceptance Thursday before receiving Allen''s Wednesday revocation, so a binding contract formed Thursday.
Wrong (B): The mailbox rule does not apply equally to both communications. Acceptance is effective on dispatch; revocation requires receipt. The mailing date of the revocation is irrelevant.
Wrong (C): Revocations do not take priority merely by being sent. A revocation is effective only when received by the offeree, not when mailed by the offeror.
Wrong (D): Not all communications are effective upon receipt. The mailbox rule specifically makes acceptances effective upon dispatch, distinguishing them from revocations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 65;

-- Q66: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Authority explicitly granted to the agent in a written agency agreement","Authority a third party reasonably believes the agent possesses based on the principal''s conduct","Authority the agent assumes on their own without the principal''s knowledge or consent","Authority conferred on the agent through a court order or judicial decree"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.03, apparent authority exists when a principal''s conduct causes a third party to reasonably believe the agent has authority. Crestwood never notified Hanover of Larson''s termination, so Hanover reasonably relied on prior dealings, binding Crestwood through apparent authority.
Wrong (A): Express authority is granted directly by the principal. Larson''s express authority was terminated in January 2026, so this does not apply.
Wrong (C): Authority assumed without the principal''s knowledge describes unauthorized action, not the third-party reliance at issue here.
Wrong (D): Court-ordered authority is not a recognized category of agency law and does not describe the Larson-Crestwood situation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 66;

-- Q67: Agency and Regulation [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Thornton is liable in both scenarios because employers are always liable for employee torts regardless of the circumstances","Thornton is liable for the delivery detour (a minor deviation within scope of employment) but not the personal errand (a frolic outside scope); Mitchell is personally liable in both","Mitchell alone is liable in both scenarios because employers are never vicariously liable for employee negligence under agency law","Thornton is liable only for the personal errand because the delivery-route accident was solely Mitchell''s fault as a deviation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 7.07, an employer is vicariously liable under respondeat superior for employee torts within the scope of employment. A 15-minute coffee detour is a minor deviation keeping Mitchell within scope, so Thornton is liable. The 30-mile personal trip on a day off is a frolic outside scope, relieving Thornton. Mitchell remains personally liable in both scenarios.
Wrong (A): Employers are not always liable. Liability under respondeat superior requires the tort to occur within the scope of employment.
Wrong (C): Employers can be vicariously liable under respondeat superior for torts committed within the scope of employment.
Wrong (D): This reverses the analysis. The delivery detour is within scope (employer liable), while the personal errand is outside scope (employer not liable).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 67;

-- Q69: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["S corporations cannot have any shareholders and operate exclusively as trusts","C corporations are pass-through entities while S corporations face double taxation","S corporation income passes through to shareholders, avoiding entity-level double taxation","C corporations are limited to a maximum of 100 shareholders under the IRC"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1366, S corporation income, losses, deductions, and credits pass through to shareholders'' individual returns, avoiding the double taxation that C corporations face under IRC Sections 11 and 301. C corporation income is taxed at the entity level and again when distributed as dividends.
Wrong (A): S corporations must have shareholders. The 100-shareholder limit under Section 1361(b)(1)(A) confirms shareholders are required.
Wrong (B): This reverses the structure. C corporations are taxed at the entity level; S corporations are pass-through entities.
Wrong (D): The 100-shareholder limit applies to S corporations under Section 1361(b)(1)(A), not to C corporations, which have no shareholder limit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 69;

-- Q71: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No difference — both gift and inheritance produce the same $55,000 gain upon immediate sale","The inheritance produces $0 gain due to stepped-up basis, while the gift produces $55,000 gain due to carryover basis","The gift produces $0 gain because gifts are always tax-free, while the inheritance produces $55,000 gain","Both produce $0 gain because transfers between family members are always fully nontaxable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1014, inherited property receives a stepped-up basis to FMV at death ($95,000), so selling at $95,000 produces $0 gain. Under Section 1015, gift property carries over the donor''s basis ($40,000), so selling at $95,000 produces $55,000 gain. This $55,000 difference illustrates the significance of the stepped-up basis at death.
Wrong (A): The two transfer methods produce fundamentally different basis outcomes — inheritance uses stepped-up basis while gifts use carryover basis.
Wrong (C): Gifts are excluded from income to the recipient but do not eliminate gain on subsequent sale. The carryover basis preserves the donor''s built-in gain.
Wrong (D): Tax treatment depends on the transfer method (gift vs. inheritance), not the family relationship. Carryover basis on gifts preserves taxable gain.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 71;

-- Q73: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Hoffman has $15,000 ordinary income under Section 1245 (full recapture); Klein has $50,000 taxed at maximum 25% as unrecaptured Section 1250 gain","Both have Section 1231 gains because business assets held over one year always qualify for capital gain treatment","Hoffman has $15,000 long-term capital gain and Klein has $50,000 ordinary income, because real property faces harsher recapture","Both have ordinary income equal to total depreciation ($35,000 and $150,000 respectively) regardless of actual gain on sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1245, Hoffman''s $15,000 gain ($80,000 minus $65,000 basis) is entirely within the $35,000 of depreciation taken, so all $15,000 is recaptured as ordinary income. Under Section 1250, Klein''s $50,000 gain on real property with straight-line depreciation is taxed as unrecaptured Section 1250 gain at a maximum 25% rate, not full ordinary income.
Wrong (B): Depreciation recapture under Sections 1245 and 1250 overrides Section 1231 treatment to the extent of prior depreciation.
Wrong (C): This reverses the relative harshness. Personal property (Section 1245) faces full ordinary recapture while real property receives the more favorable 25% maximum rate.
Wrong (D): Recapture is limited to actual gain realized, not total depreciation taken. Hoffman''s gain is only $15,000, not the full $35,000 of depreciation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 73;

-- Q77: Individual Taxation: Income [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Brennan reports in 2025 (check received and available without restriction); Sato reports in 2026 (income subject to a genuine limitation)","Both report in 2026 because income is recognized only when cash is deposited, regardless of when it becomes available","Both report in 2025 because the employer''s obligation to pay arose in 2025 for both employees","Brennan reports in 2026 when deposited; Sato reports in 2025 when promised, because constructive receipt applies to promises"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 451 and Treasury Regulation Section 1.451-2, the constructive receipt doctrine provides that income is taxable when credited, set apart, or made available without substantial limitation. Brennan had unrestricted access to the check on December 28, 2025, so income is recognized in 2025 despite depositing in January. Sato''s funds were not available until January 2026, so no constructive receipt occurred in 2025.
Wrong (B): Constructive receipt does not require actual possession or deposit. Unrestricted access to funds triggers recognition.
Wrong (C): The employer''s obligation date is irrelevant. What matters is when the income became available to each taxpayer without substantial limitation.
Wrong (D): This reverses both results. Constructive receipt requires actual or unrestricted access, not a mere promise of future payment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 77;

-- Q80: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The SALT deduction has no effect on AMT because state and local taxes are not an AMT preference item or adjustment","The $35,000 SALT addback is the primary AMT driver — it increases AMTI and pushes TMT above regular tax by $7,000","The AMT equals the full $52,000 TMT amount regardless of the taxpayer''s regular tax liability calculation","Electing not to claim the SALT deduction would eliminate AMT but always produces a worse overall tax result"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 55, AMT is the excess of tentative minimum tax over regular tax liability. Under Section 56(b)(1)(A), the SALT deduction must be added back when computing AMTI. The $35,000 SALT addback pushes TMT to $52,000, creating $7,000 in AMT ($52,000 minus $45,000 regular tax).
Wrong (A): State and local taxes are one of the largest AMT adjustment items under Section 56(b)(1)(A), directly increasing AMTI.
Wrong (C): AMT is only the excess of TMT over regular tax ($52,000 minus $45,000 = $7,000), not the full TMT amount.
Wrong (D): While forgoing the SALT deduction could reduce AMTI, it would also increase regular tax, requiring a cost-benefit analysis rather than being universally worse.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 80;

-- Q85: S Corporations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Having fewer than 50 individual shareholders on the corporate records","Issuing a second class of stock with different distribution or liquidation rights","Distributing accumulated profits to current shareholders as cash dividends","Filing a consolidated federal income tax return with a parent corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(D), an S corporation may have only one class of stock. Issuing preferred stock with different distribution or liquidation rights creates a second class, automatically terminating the S election under Section 1362(d)(2). Differences in voting rights alone do not create a second class under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1361-1(l)(1).
Wrong (A): Having fewer than 50 shareholders does not violate S corporation rules. The limit is 100 shareholders under Section 1361(b)(1)(A), and falling below any number is permissible.
Wrong (C): Distributing profits is a normal S corporation activity and does not terminate the election.
Wrong (D): S corporations cannot file consolidated returns, but the mere attempt would not terminate the election; the return would simply be rejected.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 85;

-- Q199: Circular 230 [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The fee is automatically unconscionable because it exceeds the $5,000 Circular 230 maximum for individual returns","Unconscionability uses a multi-factor test: experience, complexity, customary charges, and results obtained — not a simple market comparison","Only the client''s ability to pay determines unconscionability, regardless of the work actually performed by the practitioner","Only the practitioner''s office location and overhead costs are relevant under Circular 230''s fee proportionality rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Circular 230 Section 10.27, a practitioner may not charge an unconscionable fee. The determination uses a multi-factor test evaluating experience, reputation, novelty and difficulty of the work, customary charges in the locality, and time limitations. A $25,000 fee for a simple W-2 return is likely unconscionable because complexity is minimal and the fee vastly exceeds customary charges.
Wrong (A): Circular 230 imposes no fixed dollar cap on fees. Unconscionability is determined through the multi-factor analysis, not a threshold amount.
Wrong (C): The client''s ability to pay is not the standard. A wealthy client can still be charged an unconscionable fee if the work does not justify the amount.
Wrong (D): Office location may inform customary charges, but it is only one of several factors, not the sole determinant.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 199;

-- Q211: Business Structures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The right to manage daily operations and direct corporate employees","The right to vote on major matters, receive declared dividends, and inspect books","The right to bind the corporation in contracts with third parties","The right to transfer or encumber corporate assets at will"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under MBCA Sections 7.21 and 7.28, shareholders have the right to vote on major corporate matters (director elections, mergers, charter amendments), receive dividends when declared, and inspect books and records. These rights define the shareholder''s ownership stake without conferring management authority.
Wrong (A): Daily management is the exclusive responsibility of the board of directors under MBCA Section 8.01, not shareholders.
Wrong (C): Only authorized officers and agents can bind the corporation in contracts. Shareholders lack this authority by virtue of share ownership alone.
