-- Q251: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA personally agrees with the position, regardless of supporting legal authority","There is a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits, generally at least one-in-three","The position will definitely produce a net tax savings for the client","Another CPA has previously taken the same position on a different client''s return"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SSTS No. 1, a CPA should not recommend a tax return position unless there is a realistic possibility of being sustained on its merits, generally interpreted as at least a one-in-three (33%) likelihood. If the position does not meet this threshold, it may still be taken with a reasonable basis and adequate disclosure.
Wrong (A): Personal agreement is insufficient. The position must be supported by substantive legal authority meeting the realistic possibility standard.
Wrong (C): Potential tax savings do not determine whether a position is permissible. The standard is based on the likelihood of the position being sustained.
Wrong (D): Another CPA''s use of a position does not establish its validity. Each position must independently meet the realistic possibility threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 251;

-- Q252: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Amend the return without informing the client of the error or its consequences","Do nothing since the return has already been filed and accepted by the IRS","Report the client directly to the IRS without first discussing the matter","Promptly inform the client of the error, its consequences, and recommend corrective action"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AICPA SSTS No. 6, when a CPA discovers an error on a previously filed return still open under the statute of limitations, the CPA must promptly inform the client and recommend corrective action such as filing an amended return. The decision to correct the error remains the client''s responsibility.
Wrong (A): The CPA cannot amend the return without the client''s authorization. The client must be informed and must consent to the corrective action.
Wrong (B): Doing nothing violates the CPA''s professional obligation to notify the client of known errors on open returns.
Wrong (C): The CPA is not required to report the client to the IRS. Client confidentiality requires discussing the error with the client first.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 252;

-- Q491: Circular 230 [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both returns have identical audit risk because DIF scores are assigned randomly without regard to return characteristics","Briggs faces higher audit risk because W-2 income is more closely scrutinized than self-employment income under DIF scoring","Archer''s return likely generates a higher DIF score due to the 91% expense ratio and home office deduction, which deviate from statistical norms","Neither return faces audit risk because returns reporting under $200,000 are fully exempt from DIF scoring"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6103, the IRS Discriminant Index Function (DIF) assigns scores based on how significantly a return''s characteristics deviate from statistical norms. Archer''s 91% expense-to-revenue ratio and home office deduction deviate substantially from the expected profile for self-employment returns, producing a high DIF score.
