-- Q501: Agency and Regulation [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The investor must prove scienter under both Section 11 and Rule 10b-5 because all securities fraud claims require proving intent","Section 11 requires no scienter, reliance, or causation — only a material misstatement and damages; Rule 10b-5 requires scienter, reliance, and loss causation","Section 11 requires more elements than Rule 10b-5 because registration statements receive greater legal protection from Congress","Neither claim requires scienter because securities law imposes strict liability for all types of investor claims"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 11 of the Securities Act of 1933, the investor need only show a material misstatement in the registration statement and resulting damages — no scienter, reliance, or causation is required. The issuer is strictly liable. Under Rule 10b-5, the investor must prove scienter (intent to deceive or recklessness), materiality, reliance, causation, and damages.
Wrong (A): Section 11 does not require scienter. It imposes near-strict liability for registration statement defects, unlike Rule 10b-5 which requires proof of intent.
Wrong (C): This reverses the relative burden. Section 11 requires fewer elements than Rule 10b-5, not more.
Wrong (D): Rule 10b-5 specifically requires scienter. Only Section 11 approaches strict liability, and even then non-issuer defendants have due diligence defenses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 501;

-- Q505: Business Structures [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The creditor extended the payment deadline without the surety''s consent, causing prejudice to the surety","The principal debtor has sufficient assets to pay the full amount of the outstanding debt","The surety was not aware of the exact amount of the underlying debt obligation","The creditor failed to first demand payment from the principal debtor before pursuing the surety"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Restatement (Third) of Suretyship and Guaranty Section 37, a surety may be discharged if the creditor materially modifies the obligation — including extending the payment deadline — without the surety''s consent, to the extent the surety is prejudiced. The modification defense exists because the surety agreed to guarantee a specific obligation.
Wrong (B): The principal debtor''s solvency is not a defense for the surety. A surety is liable even if the principal debtor can pay.
Wrong (C): Lack of awareness of the debt amount is generally not a valid defense if the surety agreed to guarantee the obligation.
Wrong (D): A surety is primarily liable alongside the principal debtor. Unlike a guarantor of collection, the creditor need not pursue the principal debtor first.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 505;

-- Q506: Business Structures [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All four classes split the $800,000 pro rata because bankruptcy law treats all creditors and equity holders equally","Administrative expenses are paid first, then employee wages, then trade creditors, then equity holders receive any remainder","Equity holders are paid first because shareholders invested capital and deserve priority return of their investment","Employee wages receive first priority, then equity holders, then trade creditors, then administrative expenses last"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 507 of the Bankruptcy Code, administrative expenses (Section 507(a)(2)) receive highest priority because the system depends on professionals willing to administer the estate. Employee wages (Section 507(a)(4)) rank next, reflecting policy protecting workers. General unsecured creditors follow, and equity holders are last. Here: $100,000 admin + $150,000 wages + $400,000 trade creditors = $650,000, leaving $150,000 for equity holders (75% of $200,000).
Wrong (A): Bankruptcy uses strict priority, not pro rata across all classes. Each class must be satisfied before the next receives anything.
Wrong (C): Equity holders are the lowest priority. They accepted ownership risk and receive distributions only after all creditors are paid.
Wrong (D): This completely reverses the statutory priority order established by Section 507.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 506;

