-- Q512: Property Transactions: Basis [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The van generates larger first-year deductions because 5-year property recovers costs faster than 7-year furniture under MACRS","No difference — both assets use straight-line depreciation producing identical annual deductions under MACRS rules","The furniture produces larger deductions because 7-year property uses a more accelerated method to compensate for the longer period","Both assets produce identical deductions because MACRS assigns the same recovery period to all business personal property"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 168(e) and Rev. Proc. 87-56, MACRS classifies assets by recovery period based on estimated useful lives. Vehicles are 5-year property and furniture is 7-year property, both using 200% declining balance. Under the half-year convention per Sec. 168(d)(1), 5-year property yields a 20% first-year rate versus 14.29% for 7-year property, producing larger early deductions.
Wrong (B): Both use accelerated (200% declining balance) methods, not straight-line. The shorter recovery period for the van produces larger annual deductions.
Wrong (C): The 7-year property does not use a more accelerated method. Both classes use 200% declining balance, but the shorter 5-year period produces faster recovery.
Wrong (D): MACRS assigns different recovery periods based on asset class. Vehicles and furniture are classified differently under Rev. Proc. 87-56.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 512;

-- Q518: Property Transactions: Basis [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No meaningful difference — both methods produce the same total deduction with only different timing labels","Thornton claims larger early deductions because ADS uses accelerated rates over a longer recovery period","Thornton claims smaller annual deductions over 9 years (straight-line) while Waverly front-loads over 5 years (200% DB)","Thornton deducts the full $40,000 immediately under ADS while Waverly must spread deductions over 5 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 168(g), ADS requires straight-line depreciation over a longer recovery period (9 years vs. 5 years under GDS), producing smaller annual deductions than 200% declining balance under GDS. A company might elect ADS under Section 168(g)(7) to avoid creating unusable NOLs or to eliminate AMT depreciation adjustments.
Wrong (A): While total depreciation is identical ($40,000), the timing difference significantly affects cash flow and is not merely a labeling difference.
Wrong (B): ADS uses straight-line, not accelerated rates. ADS always produces smaller early deductions than GDS.
Wrong (D): ADS does not permit immediate expensing. It requires straight-line depreciation spread over the longer ADS recovery period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 518;

-- Q523: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Under Section 1231 alone, the $50,000 would be capital gain; Section 1245 applies only to real property and has no effect here","Under Section 1231 alone, the $50,000 would be capital gain; Section 1245 recaptures the full $50,000 as ordinary income since it does not exceed depreciation","Section 1245 converts only $30,000 to ordinary income (gain above original cost) and leaves $20,000 as Section 1231 gain","Both Sections 1231 and 1245 treat the entire $50,000 as capital gain because business property gains are always capital"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Without Section 1245, the $50,000 gain would flow through Section 1231 and potentially qualify for capital gain rates. Section 1245 overrides this by recapturing gain as ordinary income to the extent of all prior depreciation. Since the $50,000 gain is less than $80,000 of accumulated depreciation, the entire $50,000 is ordinary income. This prevents the arbitrage of deducting depreciation at ordinary rates and recognizing recovery at capital gain rates.
Wrong (A): Section 1245 applies specifically to personal property (equipment), not real property. Section 1250 governs real property recapture.
Wrong (C): Section 1245 recaptures all depreciation to the extent of gain, not just gain above original cost. The full $50,000 is recaptured.
Wrong (D): Section 1245 explicitly converts what would be capital gain into ordinary income, not the reverse.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 523;

-- Q525: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L3]
