-- Q532: Like-Kind Exchanges [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reports the gain on Schedule D in the year of the involuntary conversion event","Reinvests the proceeds in similar-use property within the statutory replacement period","Donates the full insurance proceeds to a qualified charitable organization","Elects installment sale treatment for the conversion proceeds"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1033, gain from an involuntary conversion (destruction, theft, condemnation) may be deferred if the taxpayer purchases qualified replacement property similar or related in service or use within the replacement period. The period is generally 2 years after the tax year of conversion (3 years for condemned real property).
Wrong (A): Reporting the gain on Schedule D in the conversion year is the default treatment. Deferral under Section 1033 requires reinvestment, not immediate recognition.
Wrong (C): Donating insurance proceeds to charity does not qualify as purchasing replacement property and would not defer the gain.
Wrong (D): Installment sale treatment under Section 453 applies to voluntary sales, not involuntary conversions. Section 1033 provides the applicable deferral mechanism.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 532;

-- Q533: Like-Kind Exchanges [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Henderson recognizes $20,000 gain (proceeds not reinvested); Diaz recognizes $0 because reinvestment exceeds proceeds, deferring the full gain","Henderson recognizes the full $150,000 gain and Diaz recognizes $0, because any reinvestment shortfall triggers full recognition","Both recognize $0 because Section 1033 defers all gain whenever replacement property is purchased regardless of amount","Henderson recognizes $0 and Diaz recognizes $20,000, because reinvestment exceeding proceeds creates a taxable event"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1033, gain is recognized only to the extent proceeds exceed the cost of replacement property. Henderson: $500,000 minus $480,000 = $20,000 recognized; the remaining $130,000 is deferred. Diaz: $520,000 reinvestment exceeds $500,000 proceeds, so $0 is recognized and the full $150,000 gain is deferred into a $370,000 replacement basis.
Wrong (B): Gain recognition is limited to the shortfall amount ($20,000), not the full $150,000 gain. Section 1033 provides partial deferral when reinvestment is partial.
Wrong (C): Full deferral requires reinvestment at or above the proceeds amount. Partial reinvestment triggers recognition to the extent of the shortfall.
Wrong (D): This reverses the treatment. Excess reinvestment produces no gain; it is shortfalls in reinvestment that trigger recognition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 533;

-- Q537: Like-Kind Exchanges [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both sales qualify for installment method because Section 453 applies to any sale with deferred payments","The stock sale is excluded because publicly traded securities have a readily ascertainable market value; the rental property qualifies","The stock sale qualifies for installment method but the rental property does not, because real estate is excluded from deferral","Neither sale qualifies because the installment method was repealed for all asset types by the TCJA"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 453(k)(2), sales of publicly traded securities are excluded from installment method treatment because they have a readily ascertainable market value and trade on liquid markets. The seller could receive full cash proceeds immediately, so installment deferral is not warranted. Rental property lacks a public market, making installment sales a genuine commercial necessity.
Wrong (A): Section 453(k) explicitly excludes certain asset types, including publicly traded securities, from installment method treatment.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment. Real estate is one of the most common installment method transactions, while publicly traded securities are excluded.
Wrong (D): The installment method remains available for qualifying sales. It was not repealed by the TCJA.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 537;

-- Q543: Individual Taxation: Income [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No change — Social Security benefits are always fully excluded from gross income regardless of other income","In 2025, 50% is includible (provisional income between $25,000-$34,000); in 2026, up to 85% after IRA withdrawals push past $34,000","In both years, 100% of benefits are includible because any provisional income above $25,000 triggers full inclusion","In 2025, 85% is includible and in 2026, 50% is includible, because higher income reduces the taxable percentage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 86(a) and (b), Social Security inclusion uses two provisional income thresholds for single filers: $25,000 and $34,000. In 2025, Hargrove''s $30,000 provisional income falls between thresholds, triggering up to 50% inclusion ($12,000). In 2026, IRA withdrawals push provisional income to $48,000, exceeding $34,000 and triggering up to 85% inclusion ($20,400).
Wrong (A): Social Security benefits are not always excluded. Inclusion depends on provisional income exceeding the applicable thresholds under Section 86.
Wrong (C): The inclusion percentage is 50% or 85% depending on the threshold, never 100%. Section 86 caps the maximum includible amount at 85%.
Wrong (D): This reverses the relationship. Higher provisional income increases (not decreases) the taxable percentage of Social Security benefits.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 543;

-- Q558: Individual Taxation: Credits [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both credits are available and Nguyen should claim whichever produces the higher amount for the tax year","Neither credit is available because Nguyen''s $65,000 AGI exceeds the phase-out threshold for both education credits","The AOTC is available but the LLC is not, because the AOTC covers graduate programs while the LLC is undergraduate only","The LLC is available (up to $2,000 at 20% of expenses) with no graduate or enrollment restrictions; the AOTC is unavailable for graduate study"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The AOTC under Section 25A(b) is limited to the first four years of postsecondary education, requires at least half-time enrollment, and applies only to degree programs. Nguyen''s graduate certificate program fails multiple AOTC requirements. The LLC under Section 25A(c) covers any postsecondary coursework with no enrollment intensity or year limit, generating a $1,700 credit (20% of $8,500).
Wrong (A): The AOTC is not available to Nguyen because the graduate certificate program does not meet the first-four-years and degree requirements.
Wrong (B): Nguyen''s $65,000 AGI is below the LLC phase-out threshold ($80,000 for single filers), so the LLC remains available.
Wrong (C): This reverses the credit restrictions. The AOTC is limited to undergraduate-level study, while the LLC covers graduate programs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 558;

-- Q578: S Corporations [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reclassification has no tax effect because S corporation distributions and salary are taxed at identical rates","Reclassification subjects the $120,000 to FICA taxes (Social Security and Medicare), which distributions avoid — this is why the IRS targets it","Reclassification converts the $120,000 from ordinary income to capital gains, reducing Crawford''s overall tax liability","The IRS cannot reclassify S corporation distributions because shareholders have absolute discretion over the compensation split"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 3121(a), wages are subject to FICA (Social Security at 6.2% and Medicare at 1.45% for both employer and employee), while S corporation distributions under Section 1368 are not. Reclassifying $120,000 as compensation triggers approximately $18,360 in employee FICA plus employer matching. The IRS targets zero-salary arrangements because shareholder-employees performing services must receive reasonable compensation.
Wrong (A): Distributions and salary are not taxed identically. Salary triggers FICA employment taxes that distributions avoid, creating a significant tax difference.
Wrong (C): Reclassification converts distributions to wages (ordinary income plus FICA), not capital gains. It increases, not decreases, the tax burden.
Wrong (D): The IRS can and does reclassify distributions as compensation under the reasonable compensation doctrine when shareholder-employees take no salary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 578;
